---
const rows = [
  ["under the old bridge", "Phrase", "No subject and verb making a statement."],
  ["the boat passed under the old bridge", "Clause and sentence", "boat is the subject; passed is the verb. The thought is complete."],
  ["when the boat passed under the old bridge", "Subordinate clause", "It has boat and passed, but when leaves us asking what happened then."]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Clauses - What are they?</h1>
  <p>
    A <strong>clause</strong> is a group of words built around a verb. In the examples here, it has a subject
    and a verb. Some clauses can stand alone. Others need a partner.
  </p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-mint">
    <h2>Two clauses in one sentence</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>The fox waited</strong> <strong>because the gate was closed</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>The fox waited</em> has <strong>fox</strong> + <strong>waited</strong>. <em>Because the gate was
      closed</em> has <strong>gate</strong> + <strong>was</strong>. The second does not finish the thought,
      because <em>because</em> makes us expect more.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-yellow">
    <h2>Phrase, clause or sentence?</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Words</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">What is it?</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Why?</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map(([words, kind, why]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{words}</th>
              <td>{kind}</td>
              <td>{why}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <p class="try-it">
    <strong>Try it.</strong> Turn <em>under the old bridge</em> into a sentence: “A heron stood under the old
    bridge.” Then leave it unfinished: “While a heron stood under the old bridge, …”
  </p>
</div>
